A very simple explanation of the line:

import { createRoot } from "react-dom/client";
const root = createRoot(document.getElementById("root"));
root.render(<h1>heloo</h1>);

🧠 What does this code do? (Simple Explanation)
This code is used to start a React app and show something on the screen (like a heading or component).
🔹 Step-by-step Explanation:

✅ 1. import { createRoot } from "react-dom/client";
👉 This means:
"Hey React, I want to use the tool called createRoot from your toolbox."
We use createRoot to tell React:
“Get ready to show things on the web page.”

✅ 2. document.getElementById("root")
👉 This finds the place in your HTML page where React should put the content.
Usually, your index.html has this:
<div id="root"></div>
This is the empty box where React will draw everything.

✅ 3. const root = createRoot(document.getElementById("root"));
👉 This means:
"Create a connection between React and the HTML box with id 'root'."
Now, React knows where to show things.

✅ 4. root.render(<h1>heloo</h1>);
👉 This means:
"Please show this heading: heloo inside the root box."
So, when the page loads, it will show a big bold heading:
heloo

🎯 Full Simple Example:
🧾 HTML (index.html):
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>My React App</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div id="root"></div>
    <script src="main.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
💻 JavaScript (main.js):
import React from "react";
import { createRoot } from "react-dom/client";
const root = createRoot(document.getElementById("root"));
root.render(<h1>Hello from React!</h1>);

✅ In Interview, Say Like This:
	In React 18, we use createRoot from react-dom/client to start the app.
	It connects React with the HTML div using document.getElementById("root").
	Then we use root.render() to show something like a heading or a component.
